<Section id="paths" title="4. Area Charts">

A `<path>` can draw _any_ shape. Everything lives in one attribute, `d` - a list of drawing commands the pen follows:

```svelte
/// file: A path
<path d="M 50 280 L 160 50 L 260 170 Z" fill="steelblue" />
```

- `M x y` **moves** the pen to a start point (without drawing).
- `L x y` draws a straight **line** to a point.
- `Z` **closes** the shape, joining the last point back to the start.

These strings get long and unreadable pretty fast, so in practice we **almost never write `d` by hand** - libraries like D3 generate it for us. We'll build a short one here just to see how the pen moves.

<Exercise file="3-area-chart.svelte">
	<AreaChart />
	{#snippet result()}
		<AreaDemo />
	{/snippet}
</Exercise>

{#snippet sidebar()}

One shape for the whole area:

```svelte
/// file: Path
<path
	d="M 50 280 L 160 50 L 260 170 L 360 30 L 460 230 L 460 350 L 50 350 Z"
	fill="steelblue"
	fill-opacity="0.25"
	stroke="steelblue"
	stroke-width="2"
/>
```

**RESULT**:

<AreaDemo />

---

**A COUPLE MORE PATHS**

Swap the `d` for one of these:
<br>

A **stepped area**, holding each value until the next point (only `L` commands):

```svelte
/// file: Stepped
d="M 50 280 L 160 280 L 160 50 L 260 50 L 260 170 L 360 170
   L 360 30 L 460 30 L 460 230 L 460 350 L 50 350 Z"
```


A **smooth** area, with `C` cubic curves through the points (called a **Catmull-Rom** spline). Notice how unreadable it is by hand - this is exactly what a library would generate:

```svelte
/// file: Smooth (Catmull-Rom)
d="M 50 280
   C 68 242 125 68 160 50
   C 195 32 227 173 260 170
   C 293 167 327 20 360 30
   C 393 40 443 197 460 230
   L 460 350 L 50 350 Z"
```

Or even the more elusive path of a **Cat-mulling**:
